-- WAY DECORATORS: the way the legislative style would write it, one rule per text of the Schedule, each
-- named alike and dated with an @effective decorator, as TEMPORAL-RULE-VERSION-DESIGN.md (Phase 2) describes.
--
-- The build does not know @effective: it warns, ignores the line, and then rejects the two same-named rules as
-- "multiple definitions".  So none of the pairs below is answered.  What the build prints is in
-- alt-decorators.out; walkthrough step 7.
-- (The decorator carries one date.  Even where it exists, the publication date of finding 5 has no place in it.)
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

DECLARE `Version of the First Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `the First Schedule of three items`
    `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`

@effective 2003-07-31
GIVETH A `Version of the First Schedule`
`the text, by decorator` MEANS `the First Schedule of three items`

@effective 2011-01-01
GIVETH A `Version of the First Schedule`
`the text, by decorator` MEANS `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE
      reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Version of the First Schedule`
`the text under this way` MEANS
    `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` reading (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` conduct `the text, by decorator`)

-- BEGIN GENERATED PAIRS (build-walkthrough.py: the same conduct-day and reading-day pairs in every file)
